---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import DonateForm from "@/components/DonateForm";
import { ALL, CATALOGUE_UPDATED, COMPANIES, DOCUMENTED_COUNT, FIRST_YEAR, LAST_YEAR } from "@/data/collection";
---

<Layout title="Curator's note — Merchback Machine" compact={true}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-8">
    <div class="border-b-2 border-ink pb-3">
      <p class="meta">About this archive</p>
      <h2 class="mt-1 font-display text-2xl font-bold sm:text-3xl">Curator's note</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Startup merch is the only artifact most companies produce that outlives their product. The
        code gets rewritten, the domain gets parked, the office gets subleased — and the shirt stays in
        somebody's drawer for fifteen years. The Merchback Machine catalogues that shirt.
      </p>
      <p>
        The collection currently holds <strong>{ALL.length} objects</strong> from{" "}
        <strong>{COMPANIES.length} companies</strong>, spanning {FIRST_YEAR} to {LAST_YEAR}: tees,
        hoodies, caps, socks, stickers, pins, plushies, keycaps, a hardcover imprint, a fedora, and a
        handful of things that fit no category at all.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 border-b border-ink pb-1 font-display text-lg font-bold">On dating</h3>
      <p>
        {DOCUMENTED_COUNT} of {ALL.length} records carry a <strong>documented</strong> year — the drop
        itself was publicly reported. The rest are marked <strong>estimated</strong>, and shown with a{" "}
        <span class="acc">≈</span> on every card: the year is anchored to the product, campaign or
        logo era the object belongs to, not to a verified production run. Merch is badly documented by
        nature. Nobody files a press release about a sock.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you can date one properly — a receipt, an order confirmation, a photo with a timestamp — the
        registrar wants to hear about it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 border-b border-ink pb-1 font-display text-lg font-bold">On the plates</h3>
      <p>
        Every object is shown as an illustrated <em>specimen plate</em>: a drawn reconstruction in the
        recorded colorway, not a photograph. This is deliberate. The archive holds no photography
        rights, and a drawing is honest about being an interpretation — an approach the natural history
        museums used for two centuries before cameras got cheap.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10 border-b border-ink pb-1 font-display text-lg font-bold">On scarcity</h3>
      <p>
        “Widely distributed” through “grail” is a curatorial index of how hard a piece is to get hold
        of today, not an appraisal. Nothing here is for sale and nothing here is valued. If a listing
        somewhere says otherwise, that's the market talking, not the museum.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section id="donate" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-6">
      <div class="border-b-2 border-ink pb-3">
        <p class="stamp">accession desk</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl font-bold sm:text-3xl">Donate an artifact</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Describe the object and it goes into the accession queue for the next catalogue revision.
          Corrections to existing records are just as welcome — name the accession number in the record
          field.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6">
        <DonateForm client:load />
      </div>
      <p class="meta mt-4">Catalogue revision {CATALOGUE_UPDATED}</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
